  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/8/8c/Journey_Into_Mystery_Vol_1_98.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "Don Blake/Thor is steamed about Jane Foster leaving him to work for Dr. Andrews, and Odin himself summons him to Asgard to urge him to forget the mortal.&nbsp; After only 2 panels, Thor turns his back and leaves!&nbsp; Blake decides to head out East to get away from his sorrow.\n\nWhile at the same time, we see the jealous assistant of a Professor Shecktor (who we later learn is \"Klaus,\" an ex-convict), who is close to finding an antidote for snake venom, but his assistant stages an accident - allowing a cobra to bite them both and withholding the antidote from the professor.&nbsp; But Don Blake overhears that he's dying, and being his old teacher, he decides to step in as Thor to try and help.&nbsp; However, the professor points out that the cobra was radioactive, and so like Spider-Man, there's now a human Cobra out there at large!\n\nCobra heads to the US and lands at Idlewild airport, where Thor stalks him to try and learn his plans.&nbsp; He heads to a chemical manufacturing plant and demands complete control so he can make gallons of the cobra serum \"to create a vast army of others like myself - to do my bidding and serve me without question!\" (<i>I guess he has a lot of confidence in his powers of persuasion</i>.)\n\nThor then bursts in, and we get to see a handful of Cobra's interesting abilities to hurl darts and venom in the form of gas.&nbsp; Eventually, Cobra tries to blast him with a huge rush of chemical acid-based formula, but it only enrages Thor, and at the bottom of page 9, we see Thor release a \"blood-curdling war cry\" and hurl his hammer, which destroys the vat of gas, blinding him long enough to allow the Cobra to escape.&nbsp; He heads out to find a doctor's office where he might find \"serums with which I can achieve my ultimate objective!\"&nbsp; Sure enough, it's the office of Dr. Andrews, who is there with his new nurse, Jane Foster!&nbsp; Dr. Andrews immediately caves to the Cobra's demands, but Jane Foster is incensed that he seems to show no backbone at all - he's nothing but a coward!\n\nSeeing Thor flying outside, she hurls something through the window to attract his attention, and the Cobra prepares to kill her for her insolence!&nbsp; But Thor has taken note and arrives.&nbsp; Cobra grabs the girl as a hostage and heads out the window toward the nearby bridge.&nbsp; But Thor quickly finds a way to subdue Cobra and save Jane, who immediately leaves Dr. Andrews and heads back to ask Don Blake for her old job back.\n\nThe narrative text implies that it might have been Odin's doing that drew Jane Foster back to Don Blake - \"out of love for his favorite son!\"&nbsp; The pair are together again!&nbsp; However the Cobra has escaped!",
  "grade_one_liner": "FIXME -- write something",
  "subtitle": "FIXME -- write something",
  "review_text": "The story itself is mundane, the villain derivative (in the sense that his powers stem from being bitten by a radioactive cobra), and the fact that Jane Foster is literally gone from the presence of Dr. Blake for a <i>mere one issue</i> - all are marks against this offering.\n\nOn the positive side was that one <i>awesome</i> panel that closes out page 9.&nbsp; As he hurls his hammer, Thor shouts out \"By the beard of noble Odin, oh trusty hammer - <i>strike</i>!&nbsp; Strike in the name of <i>Asgard</i>!\"&nbsp; While the panel isn't drawn so wonderfully by artist Don Heck and will never rank in the upper echelon of even mildly famous panels, it nevertheless captures the Thunder God in a moment of glory, relishing the prospect of battle!\n\nAfter years of reading Thor, I've seen these types of outcries on many occasions, but as I've been reading these old adventures from the beginning, this strikes me as the first time he has made such an outburst, so I find it notable.&nbsp; Thor strikes in the name of Asgard, even though Odin's decree about his interactions with Jane are a large part of his problems.&nbsp; Again, we're getting a peek at more of the Thor persona emerging - even this speech pattern is moving us closer to his eventual (and far more familiar) King James/Shakespeare style of speech.\n\nThat panel - and Thor thinking in passing on at least two occasions how great it is to find himself pitted against an evil foe - both serve to push the final grade on this one up a full point to just above \"reader\" level (and another half-point for the <i>Tales of Asgard</i> offering as well - see below - still not mid-grade).&nbsp; Cobra himself seems an unworthy foe for someone like Thor, but this one single shining moment (complete with battle cry) in an otherwise unremarkable tale made it completely worth the read!&nbsp; (I must point out that my first exposure to Thor was in <i>Avengers</i> 162, where we see him cry out \"Death to Ultron!&nbsp; Verily, this night we shall <i>avenge</i> our slain comrades, or taste death's bitter cup ourselves!\" - now <i>that's </i>the Thor I know!&nbsp; So I'm thrilled to see the first hint of that here in this issue!\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nA couple of side notes:\n\nThor points out in this issue that his hammer only returns to him if actually thrown.&nbsp; But in the issue, he simply drops it, where it remains motionless until retrieved.&nbsp; At the end of page 8, when he goes back to retrieve it (now as Don Blake), he \"stamps it once upon the ground\" to presumably turn back to Thor, but we've already seen before that the hammer remains a hammer even while Blake reverts to human form and that he only need touch it to become Thor again.&nbsp; So this is an inconsistency.\n\nWhen Don Blake calls Professor Shecktor his old teacher, this implies a past for Blake.&nbsp; Interesting since we find out later that the Blake persona was a made-up one by Odin.&nbsp; Will tuck this tidbit aside for comparison later.",
  "side_note": [
    "<b>Tales of Asgard</b><b> </b>introduces us to Ymir, King of the Ice Giants! (and here Jack Kirby once again is handling the artwork, as opposed to Don Heck in the main story)\n\nWe see Odin in pitched combat against a group of ice giants, but with the aid of his \"magic sword,\" he hurls them into a chasm, plunging them to \"Surtur's fiery domain!\"&nbsp; But one ice giant remains - Ymir!&nbsp; Odin's speech is full-on Asgardian, complete with the wordy outbursts we've come to expect from Thor, and it's actually quite invigorating reading it. J  \"While breath remains in me, I shall fight you!&nbsp; And by my beard, the victory shall yet be mine!\"&nbsp; (This is good stuff!)&nbsp; Odin surrounds him in a ring of fire, trapping him, and returns to his throne!\n\nNext issue, we're promised Surtur, the Fire Demon!\n\nUnlike the initial Tales of Asgard, which was largely painting a broad history, this issue actually shows Odin in action, and I sense that Stan Lee was slowly imbuing these Asgardians with a regal quality, particularly in their speech patterns.&nbsp; The narrative very much hinted that he was really getting into this style of writing.&nbsp; I very much suspect that we will soon start seeing this bleed over into the main Thor stories themselves....\n\nBottom Line: a short and mildly entertaining look at an early adventure of Odin.&nbsp; Nice to have some background material to help embellish the mythos!\n\n<b>Perhaps this book is turning the corner.&nbsp; There's certainly a hint of that here!</b>"
  ],
  "ownership": "I was fortunate enough to own two copies of this book, one of which I kept, and the other FN+ or so copy came as part of the 2000 collection, for which I paid a little over $18 and eventually sold on eBay in Feb, 2020 for $99, netting me over $68.",
